<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Obshchina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as mir, rural community</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The obshchina or mir was the peasant village community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>, made up of serfs, later emancipated serfs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-peasant">state peasants</a> and their descendants, usually of a single village. Title to the land lay with the mir, not the individual: each household farmed its own holding, which it could not sell or bequeath without the mir's consent, and the mir could repartition the land from time to time. Its assembly, the skhod, also levied recruits, punished minor crimes and answered for its members' taxes. Slavophiles hailed it as ancient and purely Russian, while the Western school of Boris Chicherin held it a later creation for tax collection; the Stolypin reforms of 1906–1914 undermined it.</p></div>
